Original commit body from 7a07a2fe864652ce751228e8cf867690c881df5a:

js_ts.py recorded a function value in an object literal ({run: h}, {h}),
a property write (obj.h = h, WI-vubal) and a call argument. Three value
positions had no edge, so a function used only there had no caller:

- a declaration, const f = handler, at module level or in a function;
- a plain assignment, f = handler;
- an array element, [a, b] (dispatch tables, parser combinators).

Each is a `references` edge with evidence function_reference, which is
not a callback registration (edge_types.is_callback_registration), so
taint does not walk it -- storing a function is not calling it, the reason
module.exports = {parse} carries the non-crossable object-literal stamp.

The pair and shorthand arms now share the new _emit_value_reference with
the three new positions (same resolution, function/method-only, npm
package boundary guard); their object_field_reference stamp is unchanged.
test_js_ts_property_assigned_function's plain-assignment test asserted no
references edge at all; it now asserts its intent (no property-write
stamp) and the one function_reference edge the binding is.

